<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>pwiz.Osprey.IO</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>Osprey.IO</AssemblyName>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Osprey.Test" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- ReSharper disable once VulnerablePackage -->
    <PackageReference Include="Parquet.Net" Version="4.25.0" />
    <!-- 1.0.119 to match pwiz-sharp, whose Bruker and UIMF readers pull the same
         package (a lower version here is an NU1605 downgrade error). -->
    <PackageReference Include="System.Data.SQLite.Core" Version="1.0.119" />
    <PackageReference Include="Newtonsoft.Json" Version="13.0.3" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Osprey.Core\Osprey.Core.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- ProteoWizard, as the ONLY spectrum reader (issue #4497). Unconditional and
       unversioned by a property: the net472 build's OspreyVendorReader opt-in
       existed because pwiz_data_cli had to be staged by a bjam build first, and
       pwiz-sharp is a managed project reference with no staging step at all.

       Direct rather than through pwiz_tools/Shared/ProteowizardWrapper, whose
       net8 target is net8.0-windows (it depends on pwiz.CommonUtil, which is
       WinForms) - a plain net8.0 project cannot reference it, and Osprey has to
       stay plain net8.0 to run on Linux without the Wine container.

       The vendor projects carry no vendor SDK unless the build opts in to the
       licenses (pwiz-sharp's IAgreeToVendorLicenses, plus NativeVendorsAvailable
       for the Windows-only native SDKs); without it they compile in
       NO_VENDOR_SUPPORT mode where Identify() works and Read() throws. So this
       reference set costs a default developer clone nothing, and one Osprey
       build runs on Linux with the managed Thermo SDK behind it. Vendor list
       matches what ProteowizardWrapper registers for Skyline - see
       SpectrumFileReader.Vendors.cs.

       KNOWN ISSUE, deliberately not papered over here: a Release Osprey builds
       and deploys the DEBUG build of these assemblies. Building a .sln unsets
       Configuration and Platform across a ProjectReference to a project that is
       not in that solution (MSBuild's ShouldUnsetParentConfigurationAndPlatform,
       on by default), and none of pwiz-sharp is in Osprey.sln. The code is
       correct either way - every gate and every byte comparison for #4497 was
       run in this state - so the cost is an unoptimized ProteoWizard doing the
       spectrum reading, not a wrong answer.

       SetConfiguration/SetPlatform metadata and clearing the property were both
       tried and BOTH MADE IT WORSE: Osprey.exe and Osprey.Test re-resolve these
       references transitively, each hop negotiating its own property set, so a
       clean Release build then produced pwiz-sharp under bin/Release, bin/Debug
       AND bin/x64 and deployed an assembly matching none of them. The fix is to
       give these projects ONE owner - put them in Osprey.sln, the way Skyline
       carries its shared projects - which is a structural change worth its own
       commit rather than a rider on the reader swap. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\pwiz-sharp\pwiz\src\MsData\MsData.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\pwiz-sharp\pwiz\src\Analysis\Analysis.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\pwiz-sharp\pwiz\src\Common\Common.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\pwiz-sharp\pwiz\src\Util\Util.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\pwiz-sharp\pwiz\src\Vendor\Thermo\Thermo.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\pwiz-sharp\pwiz\src\Vendor\Waters\Waters.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\pwiz-sharp\pwiz\src\Vendor\Sciex\Sciex.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\pwiz-sharp\pwiz\src\Vendor\Shimadzu\Shimadzu.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\pwiz-sharp\pwiz\src\Vendor\Agilent\Agilent.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\pwiz-sharp\pwiz\src\Vendor\Bruker\Bruker.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\pwiz-sharp\pwiz\src\Vendor\UIMF\UIMF.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\pwiz-sharp\pwiz\src\Vendor\UNIFI\UNIFI.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- DotNetZip (Ionic.Zlib) for stock-zlib-compatible deflate output.
       Matches Skyline's BlibData writer (Util/Extensions/UtilDB.Compress)
       so Osprey .blib peak blobs are byte-identical to Skyline's
       and to Rust osprey's flate2 output. .NET's built-in DeflateStream
       produces a 4-byte-shorter non-stock-zlib variant which would split
       cross-impl byte parity. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Reference Include="DotNetZip">
      <HintPath>..\..\Shared\Lib\DotNetZip\DotNetZip.dll</HintPath>
    </Reference>
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
